WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:01.580
A café table.

2
00:00:01.630 --> 00:00:03.300
Two people talking.

3
00:00:03.350 --> 00:00:07.790
Picture their voices as coloured
bars sliding along a timeline.

4
00:00:07.840 --> 00:00:09.400
One bar runs on.

5
00:00:09.450 --> 00:00:13.440
Then the other cuts in, and
for a moment the two overlap.

6
00:00:13.490 --> 00:00:14.980
Was that an interruption?

7
00:00:15.030 --> 00:00:16.550
Or was it agreement?

8
00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:19.400
People often say men interrupt more.

9
00:00:19.450 --> 00:00:21.570
But how would you even test that?

10
00:00:21.620 --> 00:00:26.660
In this mission, you'll meet four competing
ways of looking at gender and talk.

11
00:00:26.710 --> 00:00:32.280
You'll examine a famous study from 1975
that set out to count interruptions.

12
00:00:32.330 --> 00:00:35.040
And you'll weigh up what
a fair test would need,

13
00:00:35.090 --> 00:00:37.470
before you trust anyone's count.

14
00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:40.750
Your mission: decide
how you would test it.
